      "Company records often reveal where value went. Sales of property or stock to connected parties, large payments to related entities, unusual director loan account movements, and assets transferred to a new company shortly before collapse (sometimes called phoenixing) all leave a documentary trail.",
      "We review the books and records provided to the office-holder, supplement them with registry, litigation and open-source research, and prepare a timeline of significant events. We also map relationships between the failed company, its officers and any successor entities.",
      "Overseas elements frequently arise. A parent company, bank account or property abroad may need recognition proceedings under the Cross-Border Insolvency Regulations 2006 or local equivalents, and local counsel will be involved. Early identification of such elements reduces delay.",
      "Our reports are written so that they can be understood by a court. We highlight what is established, what is inferred, and what needs further inquiry by compulsory process. We cannot guarantee recoveries, because many insolvent estates simply have little left, but a disciplined investigation gives creditors the best informed starting point."
