      "The past year has brought significant developments in UK economic crime law. Though much of the commentary is aimed at large corporates, several changes have practical consequences for individuals and smaller firms involved in disputes or recoveries.",
      "The Economic Crime and Corporate Transparency Act 2023 continues to take effect in stages. The failure to prevent fraud offence, due to apply from September 2025, places a reasonable-procedures duty on large organisations. An expanded identification doctrine also makes it easier to attribute criminal liability to companies through senior managers.",
      "## Companies House reforms",
      "Companies House now has greater powers to query and remove inaccurate information, and identity verification for directors and persons with significant control is being introduced. For investigators, this should over time improve the reliability of the public record, although transitional gaps and historic data will remain a concern.",
      "The overseas entities register and the continuing use of unexplained wealth orders have also shaped how property-related cases are approached. Civil litigants should not expect state agencies to pursue their private claims, but the greater transparency is a useful resource for lawful research.",
      "On the crypto side, the FCA's financial promotions regime and the move towards a comprehensive framework for cryptoassets mean that more firms will be regulated and, in principle, more able to cooperate with legal process.",
      "None of this removes the need for sound professional advice. If you are concerned about how these developments affect a dispute or a transaction, your solicitor should be the first port of call. We are happy to explain what lawful investigation can add alongside them."
