      "One of the first questions we are asked is how long a worldwide asset search will take and what it will cost. The honest answer is that it depends on who, where and why, and that a good investigator will talk through those variables before accepting an instruction.",
      "The starting data matters. A full name, date of birth, known addresses and a list of companies make for a focused search. A first name and a rumour make for a long one. Common names and transliterated names increase the effort needed to avoid mistaken identity, which is a risk we take seriously.",
      "## What drives scope",
      "The number of jurisdictions is the largest factor. Some countries have open and searchable registries of companies and land, whereas others require local agents, physical attendance or formal requests. Translation and certification add time. Where a register simply does not exist, we say so rather than inventing leads.",
      "We usually recommend a phased approach. A first phase of desktop research over one or two weeks establishes the structure of the subject's interests and identifies promising jurisdictions. A second phase deepens the work where justified, and the client can stop at any point once they have what they need.",
      "Costs are agreed in writing in advance, with clear points at which scope is reviewed. We think it is irresponsible to offer a no-win, no-fee arrangement that might give an investigator an incentive to overstate what has been found.",
      "Finally, the findings must be usable. If the aim is litigation or enforcement, we work with your solicitors from the beginning so that evidence is gathered lawfully and in the form needed by a court. No investigator can promise to locate assets, but a structured approach gives you the best chance of an informed decision."
